      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The stochastic properties of turbulent airflow during fricative production are governed by the interaction of high-velocity laminar flow with a constriction, leading to a breakdown of the boundary layer and the generation of aperiodic noise. This process is fundamentally a function of the Reynolds number ($Re$), defined as $Re = \\frac{\\rho U D}{\\mu}$, where $\\rho$ is air density, $U$ is flow velocity, $D$ is the characteristic dimension of the constriction, and $\\mu$ is dynamic viscosity. As $Re$ exceeds the critical threshold for transition (typically $Re > 2000$ in narrow channels), the flow becomes unstable, generating vortices that cascade into the broadband noise spectrum observed in the acoustic signal.\n\nThe spectral density of this turbulence is directly influenced by the geometry of the vocal tract, specifically the cross-sectional area gradient and the length of the constriction. A narrower aperture increases the velocity for a given volumetric flow rate, thereby elevating the Reynolds number and shifting the spectral centroid toward higher frequencies. Furthermore, the shape of the constriction determines the distribution of turbulent kinetic energy; a sharp-edged or slit-like aperture promotes earlier transition and a flatter spectral",
      "source": "target",
