      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The manifestation of audible noise from micro-scale turbulent eddies is a direct consequence of the inertial subrange in the energy cascade, where energy transferred from large-scale eddies down to smaller scales is dissipated into heat via viscous forces at the Kolmogorov scale. When the characteristic size of these eddies ($\\eta$) approaches the wavelength of sound ($\\lambda$) within the vocal tract, the pressure fluctuations generated by the unsteady separation of the boundary layer couple with the acoustic field, creating the broadband spectrum characteristic of fricatives. The perceived \"texture\" or roughness of the sound correlates with the rate of energy dissipation across the inertial subrange; higher dissipation rates, driven by increased Reynolds numbers and sharper constriction geometries, result in a denser distribution of high-frequency components, which the auditory system interprets as increased spectral flatness or \"hissiness.\"\n\nThis phenomenon is mathematically described by the Kolmogorov -1941 theory, which posits that the energy spectrum $E(k)$ follows a power law $E(k) \\propto k^{-5/3}$ in the inertial range, where $k$ is the wavenumber. The",
