      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The transition from laminar to turbulent flow is not a singular event but a continuous evolution of instability, characterized by the growth of perturbations that eventually saturate into fully developed turbulence. The \"character\" of the resulting noise is defined by the temporal autocorrelation of the pressure fluctuations, which decays exponentially with a time constant inversely proportional to the convective velocity and the size of the eddies. This decay rate determines the spectral slope; a faster decay corresponds to a steeper high-frequency roll-off, while a slower decay yields a flatter spectrum extending further into the audible range.\n\nRegarding the onset, the signal does not exhibit a sharp, discontinuous step-function rise typical of a square wave. Instead, the perceived onset is a gradual increase in the root-mean-square (RMS) amplitude of the pressure signal as the Reynolds number crosses the critical threshold for transition. This gradual buildup reflects the time required for the initial disturbances to amplify and cascade down the inertial subrange. The irregularity of the airflow pattern, quantified by the intermittency factor, correlates with the perceived \"grittiness\" or \"roughness\" of the fricative; higher intermittency values indicate more frequent bursts of high-energy vortices, which",
